   'Measured change in worker productivity from AI tools', 'Effect sizes from six controlled studies of workers using AI on the job, by occupation, published 2025–26', '% change in the study''s productivity measure',
   '%', 0, 'category',
   'Six separate academic studies collected in one AI Index table — not a common measure, so the bars are not strictly comparable. Each measures something different: output volume for authors, completed pull requests for developers, issues resolved per hour for support agents, task speed for the METR study. The -19% METR result is a speed penalty for experienced open-source developers that the same team could not replicate later. A seventh row, customer support agents at +14%–15%, is a range in the report and is left out rather than picking a point value.', 'p. 49–50 (report p. 219–220)', 'Customer support agents using a conversational AI assistant resolved 14%–15% more issues per hour (Brynjolfsson et al., 2025), software developers using GitHub Copilot completed 26% more pull requests (Cui et al., 2025), and marketing teams using multimodal AI for ad creation saw a 50% increase in output per worker (Ju and Aral, 2025).',
   11)
